part.text:
Investigation complete. I did not read `test15.cpp`; everything below comes from disassembly (gdb/objdump) plus live black-box probes.

## Program interface (recovered from `main` disassembly)

```
test15_executable <scheme> <host> <port> <path> <query> <fragment>
```

- `argc < 7` → exit `1`, no stdout/stderr. Extra args (argc > 7) are silently ignored.
- On success it prints 8 lines to stdout and exits `0`: `scheme`, `host`, `port` (as int), `path`, `query`, `fragment`, `to_string()`, `is_valid()` (as `0`/`1`).
- `argv[3]` is parsed with `std::stoi` (disassembly shows raw `strtol(base=10)` + errno/endptr/int-range checks = libstdc++ `std::stoi`), then `url_parser::URL::from_components(scheme, host, int_port, path, query, fragment)` is called.

## Class 1: `std::stoi` port-string parsing (main)

| Input class | Example | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Plain decimal | `8080`, `0`, `65535` | parsed, exit 0 |
| Leading whitespace | `"  42  "`, `" \t\n\v\f\r 7"` | all 6 C `isspace` chars skipped; parsed |
| Non-C whitespace | NBSP `"\xc2\xa05"`, full-width `"５"` | `std::invalid_argument` → abort |
| Sign | `+80`→80, `-0`→0, `-1`→-1 | parsed |
| Sign w/o digits / bare sign / double sign | `-`, `+`, `- 5`, `--5`, `++5` | `std::invalid_argument` → abort |
| Empty / whitespace-only | `""`, `" "` | `std::invalid_argument` → abort |
| Partial numeric suffix (junk ignored, `pos` unchecked) | `80abc`→80, `1e3`→1, `0x10`→0, `42 x`→42, `5-5`→5, `" 9 9"`→9 | parsed, exit 0 |
| Leading zeros | `010`→10, `+00080`→80, `00065535`→65535 | decimal, not octal |
| int range boundary | `2147483647`, `-2147483648` | OK (parsed long, fits int) |
| Out of int range | `2147483648`, `-2147483649`, `±9223372036854775807/8...`, `99999999999999999999999999` | `std::out_of_range` → abort |

Failure mode for both exceptions (uncaught): **exit status 134 (SIGABRT, signal 6)**, empty stdout, stderr exactly:
```
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'   (or 'std::out_of_range')
  what():  stoi
```

## Class 2: `from_components` / `set_port` normalization

Disassembly of `set_port(int)`: `if (p < 0 || p > 65535) port_ = -1; else port_ = p;` (field default is also -1; `-1` = "no port").

- `0..65535` → kept verbatim (`0` is a valid, printed port: `s://h:0/...`).
- `< 0` (e.g. `-1`, `-65536`, `-2147483648`) or `> 65535` (e.g. `65536`, `65536x`, `2147483647`) → normalized to `-1`.
- Port line prints `-1`; `to_string()` omits the `:port` segment entirely. This is **not** an error — exit 0, no stderr.
- `is_valid()` = `!host.empty() || !path.empty()` (set by `from_components`); it is completely independent of the port value — port `-1` still yields `1` if host or path is nonempty.

## `to_string()` reconstruction (context for port output)

- If scheme nonempty: `scheme + "://"`, then if host nonempty: host, then if `port != -1`: `":" + port`.
- If scheme is **empty**, scheme/host/port are all skipped (even a valid port is not rendered).
- Then path (unconditionally), `"?"+query` if nonempty, `"#"+fragment` if nonempty.
- Verified: `[http "" 80 "" q f]` → `http://:80?q#f`, valid `0`; `["" h 80 "" "" ""]` → to_string `""`, valid `1`.

## Rust-compatible specification

```rust
// CLI
if args.len() < 7 { process::exit(1); }          // extra args ignored
let (scheme, host, port_s, path, query, fragment) = (&a[1], &a[2], &a[3], &a[4], &a[5], &a[6]);

// std::stoi equivalent (base 10, no full-consumption check)
fn stoi(s: &str) -> Result<i32, StoiErr> {
    let b = s.as_bytes();
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' '|b'\t'|b'\n'|b'\x0b'|b'\x0c'|b'\r') { i += 1; }
    let neg = match b.get(i) { Some(b'+') => {i+=1; false}, Some(b'-') => {i+=1; true}, _ => false };
    let start = i;
    let mut v: i64 = 0; // strtol accumulates as i64 (long), clamps to i64::MIN/MAX with ERANGE
    let mut erange = false;
    while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
        v = v*10 + (b[i]-b'0') as i64;             // on i64 overflow: clamp to i64::MAX (or MIN if neg), erange = true
        i += 1;
    }
    if i == start { return Err(StoiErr::InvalidArgument); }   // no digits (incl. empty, ws-only, bare sign)
    if neg { v = -v; }
    if erange || v < i32::MIN as i64 || v > i32::MAX as i64 { return Err(StoiErr::OutOfRange); }
    Ok(v as i32)                                    // trailing junk ignored
}
// On Err: eprintln "terminate called after throwing an instance of
// 'std::invalid_argument'|'std::out_of_range'\n  what():  stoi"; abort via SIGABRT (exit 134).

// from_components / set_port normalization
let port: i32 = if (0..=65535).contains(&p) { p } else { -1 };
let is_valid = !host.is_empty() || !path.is_empty();

// to_string
let mut s = String::new();
if !scheme.is_empty() {
    s += scheme; s += "://";
    if !host.is_empty() { s += host; }
    if port != -1 { s += &format!(":{}", port); }
}
s += path;
if !query.is_empty()    { s += "?"; s += query; }
if !fragment.is_empty() { s += "#"; s += fragment; }

// output: scheme, host, port, path, query, fragment, to_string, is_valid as 0/1 — each + "\n"; exit 0
```

Ambiguity note for the caller: the Rust `stoi` emulation must replicate `strtol`'s i64 clamping, but both the ERANGE and int-range paths converge on the same `out_of_range` abort, so a simpler "parse prefix digits into i128 / checked i64, then range-check against i32" produces identical observable behavior. Exit-134/SIGABRT with the two-line stderr message is the only observable failure channel; there is no in-band error output.
